{
  "version": 203,
  "released": "2026-08-24",
  "changelog": "v203: \"that never happened\" is a finished answer. WHY: on 2026-08-24 the founder Played the Military landmark on the hosted Timeline and answered plainly — \"I have not served in the military\" — the message filed, and the Military row stayed in the open Landmarks list, where it would have stayed forever. ROOT CAUSE, two layers deep. (1) Nothing was recorded: the turn instructions told the model to emit a `landmark` only when \"the USER actually gave you one\", and a negative reads as nothing to give — the vault's state/landmarks.json shows one record, `birth`, and no military row at all, while the answer itself landed as an ordinary story source. (2) Even if it HAD been recorded there was no rung for it: military's ladder is happened -> branch -> span with complete_at: span, so a \"no\" could at best reach `happened`, two rungs below target, and status_for_domain would still have said `partial`. The same hole sat under partnerships, children and losses: a person with none of a thing could not finish that domain, which is exactly the shape that makes an instrument read as a form. FIX: a first-class NONE TERMINAL. The record is {\"domain\": …, \"none\": true}; rung_reached reports the domain's own complete_at for it, and that is the ONLY place the rule lives — status_for_domain returns `complete`, next_rung returns None, open_landmarks drops the row, and any host already rendering \"the rows that are not complete\" hides it without learning a new word. It is deliberately NOT a fourth status value, for that reason. WHICH DOMAINS: derived from the question set rather than declared beside it — the four whose ladder OPENS at `happened` (partnerships, children, military, losses), which is landmarks_interaction.domain_accepts_none. v202's ninth domain `family` is deliberately NOT one of them: its ladder opens at `who`, not `happened`, because it is an ENUMERATION of people and \"no siblings\" does not mean there was no family — a family chain is finished the way every chain is, with chain_complete, and the derivation gate reaches that conclusion on its own with no new rule. A none asserted on birth, family, residences, schools or work is dropped: {\"domain\": \"birth\", \"none\": true} would complete the axis with no date and take chronology.from_age down with it. SUPERSESSION: merge_landmark_entry is now the one definition of how two filed records combine, and timeline.save_landmark calls it — normally a merge so later rungs land on one entry; a none REPLACES what was there; any substantive answer CLEARS a standing none. \"Actually I did serve, briefly\" reopens the domain at the rung the new answer reaches: the none is superseded, not fought, and behavior.md says to take that without pointing out they told you otherwise. A NONE IS NOT A SKIP: a skip is \"not now\" and files nothing (landmark_invocation still returns None for it); a none is \"there is nothing here\" and files, through the same one writer, as `landmark-record <domain> --none`. When the model cannot tell which it heard, the instruction is to treat it as a skip — asking once more is a small cost, recording someone as childless because they changed the subject is not. SURFACES: questions.yaml documents the derivation; prompt/turn-instructions.md and prompt/behavior.md tell the model both the rule and the skip/none distinction, including the case that actually occurred (a no for the domain asked, with something adjacent volunteered in the same breath — the adjacent thing is a story, not a landmark for a domain nobody mentioned); conversation_delivery's `landmark` output contract gains \"none\": true | false and its note, still gated on TurnShape.landmark_stage so a passive user's turn contract is byte-identical. Goldens: landmarks-none-is-a-finished-answer, landmarks-none-is-superseded-not-fought, landmarks-none-completes-children. Tests: tests/test_landmarks.py (NoneTerminalTests, NoneSupersessionTests, NoneCliTests), written in the live shape. SECOND FIX, FOUND IN THE SAME LOOK AND THE SAME DEFECT: the ladder's FIRST rung was unreachable in practice. Ten minutes after the military answer the same person answered `children` in full — four names and a span — and that row ALSO stayed open, because the model filled `label` and `span` and nothing ever filled `happened`, so rung_reached returned None for a fully answered domain and status_for_domain said `partial` from rung zero. `happened` is not a fact anyone states separately; it is entailed by every other fact in the domain — you cannot name your children without having children. landmarks_interaction.asserts_happened now satisfies that rung from anything else the entry carries, on the READ side deliberately, so records already sitting in real vaults are healed the next time they are read with no migration and no rewrite. It does not manufacture completeness: that children record still reads `partial`, because `who`/`year`/`month` are genuinely missing — it just stops reading as if nothing had been said at all. Passive users are untouched: no state schema changed, no existing record shape changed, and a vault with no none records behaves exactly as v202. REBASED onto v202 (family-landmark, lifehug#210), which landed lifehug#207's date-grain fix in the same `rung_reached` loop: the two are unioned there — the date-grain rungs resolve from the record's granularity, `happened` resolves from anything else in the entry, and the none terminal short-circuits the walk before either.",
